1. c4 b5
{ The Yananish Gambit - a very strange, very rare opening. On first glance it makes no sense because white wins a clean pawn if white decides to take. The idea is similar to the Benko and Sicilian gambits - black is fighting for control over d5. When white plays the English, white wants to control d5 with the c pawn, knight c3, and bishop g2. }
( 1... b5 { 413 } 2. cxb5 a6 3. bxa6
{ White should NOT take on a6. If you take on a6, black recaptures with the bishop and you have the Benko situation where if you ever move your e pawn forward (which you'll want to because black will play d5), black takes on f1, misplacing your king. This is exactly what black wants. }
( 3. e3
{ The best move for white - simply defending the b pawn and continuing own development instead of helping black out. If black doesn't take now, white takes on a6. Play e3 to force black to take, speeding up your own development as opposed to giving black extra developing moves. }
3... axb5
{ Black should take. If black doesn't take, white takes on a6, and if black recaptures with the bishop, white can trade that bishop off and the king is still safely on e1. }
4. Bxb5
{ White recaptures with the bishop. Unlike the line where white takes on a6 first, here white's king is still on e1 and can safely castle. }
4... c6 { Best move. The game that was played continued this way. White wins. }
